--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A texture is just another image — but instead of drawing on it, you read colours from it.

  // Screen (you write to this)
  game->img  = mlx_new_image(...)      // blank paper
  game->addr = mlx_get_data_addr(...)  // pen to write pixels

  // Texture (you read from this)
  game->tex[0].img  = mlx_xpm_file_to_image(...)  // load image from file
  game->tex[0].addr = mlx_get_data_addr(...)       // pointer to read pixels

  The only difference: the screen image is blank (you draw on it every frame), the texture
  image is pre-loaded from the XPM file (you only read from it).

Parsing → stores the file path string into game->tex[i].path
  Loading → reads that file from disk into memory (game->tex[i].img + game->tex[i].addr)
  Rendering → reads pixel colours from game->tex[i].addr and draws them on screen

  parse()         → game->tex[i].path — the file path your parser stored, e.g. "./src/textures/north.xpm"
  load_textures() → game->tex[0].img  = (image loaded from that file)
                    game->tex[0].addr = (pointer to pixels of that image)
  render_frame()  → reads from tex[0].addr, writes to game->addr

  Loading must happen after mlx_init() (because MLX needs to be started first) and before
  mlx_loop() (because rendering starts there).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

How to get colour from addr?

mlx_xpm_file_to_image — Loading the image

  Think of your XPM file as a recipe book sitting on a shelf. mlx_xpm_file_to_image picks up
  that book, reads it, and cooks the actual meal (image) in memory.

  XPM file on disk          →       Image in RAM
  "./src/textures/north.xpm"  →   game->tex[0].img
  (recipe book on shelf)      →   (cooked meal in kitchen)

  width and height get filled automatically because MLX reads the first line of the XPM:
  "64 64 1 1"   ← MLX reads this → width=64, height=64

  ---
  mlx_get_data_addr — Getting access to pixels

  Once the image is in memory, you need a way to reach individual pixels. Think of the image
  as a very long corridor of rooms (pixels), each room storing one colour.

  mlx_get_data_addr gives you the key to room 0 (the addr pointer), plus 3 rules to navigate
  to any room:

  ---
  bpp — bits per pixel (usually 32)

  Each pixel stores one colour. That colour takes up 32 bits = 4 bytes of memory.

  1 pixel = 4 bytes:
  [ Alpha ] [ Red ] [ Green ] [ Blue ]
    1 byte   1 byte   1 byte    1 byte

  So bpp = 32 means every pixel occupies 4 bytes. bpp / 8 = 4 converts to bytes.

  ---
  line_len — bytes per row

  The image is stored as one long flat array in memory, NOT as a 2D grid. All rows are
  placed one after another:

  Memory: [row0_pixel0][row0_pixel1]...[row0_pixel63][row1_pixel0][row1_pixel1]...

  line_len tells you how many bytes one full row takes. For a 64px wide texture with 32bpp:
  line_len = 64 pixels × 4 bytes = 256 bytes per row

  You need this to jump from one row to the next.

  ---
  endian — byte order

  Each pixel is 4 bytes. endian tells you which byte comes first in memory:

  endian = 0 (little-endian):  [Blue][Green][Red][Alpha]   ← x86 Mac/Linux
  endian = 1 (big-endian):     [Alpha][Red][Green][Blue]

  MLX handles this for you internally — you usually don't need to worry about it.

  ---
  How to reach any pixel using these 3 values

  To read the colour of pixel at position (x, y) inside a texture:

  addr  →  start of pixel memory (room 0)
  + y * line_len  →  skip y rows down
  + x * (bpp / 8)  →  skip x pixels across

  colour = *(int *)(addr + y * line_len + x * (bpp / 8))

  Visual example for pixel at (2, 1) in a 64px wide texture:

  Row 0: [px0][px1][px2]...[px63]       ← skip this entire row (256 bytes)
  Row 1: [px0][px1][px2]...[px63]       ← land here, then skip 2 pixels (8 bytes)
                    ↑
                read this pixel

  offset = 1 * 256 + 2 * 4 = 264 bytes from start
  colour = *(int *)(addr + 264)

  ---
  Comparing screen vs texture

  ┌────────────┬───────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────┐
  │            │      Screen (game->addr)      │  Texture (tex[i].addr)  │
  ├────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┤
  │ Created by │ mlx_new_image                 │ mlx_xpm_file_to_image   │
  ├────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┤
  │ Purpose    │ You write pixels to it        │ You read pixels from it │
  ├────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┤
  │ Formula    │ Same y * line_len + x * bpp/8 │ Same formula            │
  ├────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┤
  │ Updated    │ Every frame                   │ Once at startup         │
  └────────────┴───────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────┘

  Both are just flat arrays of pixels in memory — the only difference is read vs write.

 The key insight is: addr is just a flat array, and bpp/line_len are the rules to navigate it.

